"Jesssica" <implicitlydefined@gmail.com> writes:

> Is it better to delve as deep into the dungeon as possible, going
> downstairs whenever you find a staircase, or to explore every level
> before going down further?

It depends on the playing style, but I believe many people would agree
with me here:

When you find stairs down, go down and then come back. This is because
many monsters on the level get created when the level is created, and the
diffiiculty level of those monsters depend partly on the dungeon level,
partly on your explevel. 
After visiting downstairs, explore the rest of the level you were on. Kill
the hostile monsters (ones that you can handle) and check for useful
equipment. When there are no more enemies, there's no point in staying on
a cleared level, so go down to explore the next level.

If you find some monster too tough, it may be a good idea to retreat to
earlier levels to rest and see if some monster with some more useful
equipment will be created there.

There are different opinions on when to explore the Mines and Sokoban.
Normally when I find the stairs to the Mines, I don't dive down there
immediately (of course it may take some steps on the first level to find
out you ARE in the Mines), but go back to the main dungeons instead, down
to Oracle and the next level below. Then I solve the Sokoban and go back
and explore the Mines.

